WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.840
Changes in your diet not
only affect you physically,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.760
physiologically inside,

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.520
but also mentally,
how well you think,

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.040
psychologically,
how well you feel.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.160
But you’ll never know just
how good you can feel

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:18.960
until you put it to the test
and try eating healthy.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.240
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.940
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.380
Today we look at the not so
sweet side of sweeteners

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.200
and we start with
one called erythritol.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.780
It’s funny, when you search
for information

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.300
about the low-calorie
sweetener erythritol

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.080
in the scientific literature,
you stumble across papers like this,

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.400
describing a use for erythritol
that I had not imagined.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.860
But you think that’s explosive?

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.860
What about headlines like these?

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.520
Erythritol associated with increased
body fat in young adults,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.400
metabolic dysfunction,
coronary heart disease,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.600
heart failure, pre-diabetes,
full-blown diabetes,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.160
and diabetic complications
like kidney damage and blindness.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.580
But wait,
I thought erythritol

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:11.100
has been reported
to be a totally safe

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.920
and even have
anti-oxidant properties.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.220
Let’s take a closer look
at these studies.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.340
Upon entering college,
many young adults

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.860
experience weight gain—
the dreaded “freshman 15”—

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.400
which can start them off
on the wrong course in life

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.020
in terms of developing
chronic illness.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.480
But not all freshmen do;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.880
so, researchers wanted to try
to tease out why some

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.440
gained weight
and others didn’t.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.960
So, on one of their
first days at school

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.120
a bunch of students
got their blood drawn,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.080
and then nine months later

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.240
were reweighed
and got more blood taken.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.000
About a quarter of the students
maintained a stable weight,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.300
but the other three quarters gained
an average of about nine pounds.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.880
Then, they did something
interesting.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.940
They pooled the blood of all
those who remained stable

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.800
and compared that
to the collection

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.060
of blood from all
the gainers

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.280
to see if they could
pick out any differences.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.760
And the ones who gained weight
had significantly higher levels

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.380
of erythritol in their blood,

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.680
and not just by a little—
15 times higher levels.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.080
Those found to have poor
blood sugar control

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:13.260
had 21 times higher
blood erythritol levels.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.080
And the same thing
with heart disease.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.420
What did people
who developed heart disease

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.160
have more in their blood
than those that didn’t?

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:21.390
Erythritol!

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.420
The same thing with diabetes
and diabetes complications.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.360
Okay, so what’s going on?

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:31.380
Is erythritol contributing to obesity,
and heart disease, and diabetes,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.420
or is it just
an innocent bystander?

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.240
Maybe it’s reverse causation,

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.940
meaning instead of erythritol
leading to more weight gain,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.460
maybe weight gain
led to more erythritol.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.820
After all, who tends to go
for the low-calorie sweeteners?

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.620
But wait a second.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.480
In this study, for example,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.900
they took blood before
and after a period of over 20 years.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.780
Was erythritol
even around back then?

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:57.060
The first blood draws
were in the 80s,

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.840
and erythritol wasn’t
approved until 2001.

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.820
Same thing with
the heart disease study.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:05.400
The blood samples
were taken in the late 80s;

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:09.720
so, the presence of erythritol
in their blood cannot be explained

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.060
by consumption of erythritol
as a sugar replacement.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.260
So, wait.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.520
How did they end up

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:16.320
with so much erythritol
in their system?

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.920
Their own body made it.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:23.280
We know that your body
can take excess blood sugar

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:25.560
and convert it into erythritol.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.200
If you give people
radioactively labeled sugar,

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:29.160
within two hours

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.880
radioactively labeled erythritol
shows up in your blood.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.160
It turns out erythritol

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:37.620
is a pentose-phosphate
pathway metabolite,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.020
meaning that’s the pathway
by which blood sugar

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.880
is converted into erythritol.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.600
And the pentose-phosphate pathway
is a protective pathway.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:48.480
It's a way your body disposes
of excess blood sugar

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.760
by trying to convert
the sugar to other things

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:52.500
to bring down blood sugar levels.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.540
So, having erythritol
in your system

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.580
seems to be a consequence
of too high sugars, not the cause.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.120
So, no wonder erythritol
is associated with diabetes.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.480
That may be just like saying
high blood sugar levels

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.180
are associated with
high blood sugar levels.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.940
And high blood sugars
are also associated

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.100
with obesity and heart disease.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.640
In fact, erythritol synthesis
could be an adaptive mechanism

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.380
to counter the oxidative stress

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:21.900
induced by obesity
and high blood glucose.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.720
In other words, not just
a way to reduce blood sugar,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.240
but since erythritol
can act as an antioxidant,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.980
maybe your body
is making it

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:31.440
specifically to help
sop up the mess.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.440
The bottom line is that
there are no studies

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.780
linking the consumption
of erythritol

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:37.920
to any disease outcomes.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.200
But the flip side
of that is yeah,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.780
the studies that have been
done on erythritol do show

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.900
it to be relatively innocuous
and possibly beneficial,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.660
but the effects of chronic
erythritol consumption

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:51.780
have not been evaluated
either way.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.400
That’s where the science was,

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.420
until 2023, when a prestigious lab
out of Cleveland Clinic

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.720
found increased clotting
susceptibility in human blood

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.240
in a test tube and mouse
blood in a mouse artery.

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:08.340
That was at blood concentrations
like 45 micromoles, or even 290.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.220
But have people chug two
tablespoons of the stuff

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.120
and erythritol blood levels
jump up into the thousands

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.020
within a matter of minutes.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.320
And so, until we have clinical
studies exonerating the stuff,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:23.754
I would recommend people to
stay away from erythritol.

00:05:24.060 --> 00:05:27.649
Next up, we look at the conflicting
data on the intake of aspartame,

00:05:27.649 --> 00:05:29.160
sold as Nutrasweet,

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.320
with respect to non-
Hodgkin’s lymphoma,

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.880
multiple myeloma,
leukemia,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:34.554
and pancreatic cancer.

00:05:34.554 --> 00:05:37.800
The approval of aspartame
has a controversial history.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.580
The FDA Commissioner concluded that
there was reasonable certainty

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:43.440
that human exposure to aspartame

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:45.960
would not pose a risk
of brain damage

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:49.080
resulting in mental retardation,
hormonal dysfunction, or both;

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.000
and will not cause
brain tumors,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.940
however, the FDA’s own
Public Board of Inquiry

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.120
withdrew their approval
over cancer concerns.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.420
Furthermore, several
FDA scientists advised

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:01.560
against the approval,

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:05.460
citing the Aspartame company’s
own brain tumor tests.

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:08.100
The Commissioner approved
aspartame anyway,

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.460
before he left the FDA and enjoyed
a thousand dollar a day

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:16.200
consultancy position with the
aspartame company’s PR firm.

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.700
Then the FDA actually prevented the
National Toxicology Program (NTP)

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.560
from doing further
cancer testing,

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:26.100
so we were left with people battling
over different rodent studies,

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.320
some of which showed
increased cancer risk

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:29.820
and some
that didn’t.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.980
Reminds me of the whole
saccharin story,

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:34.680
where it caused bladder
cancer in rats, but not in mice,

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.620
leaving us with
unanswerable questions like,

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.140
"So, are we more
like a rat or a mouse?"

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.180
We obviously had to put the aspartame
question to the test in people,

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:48.960
but the longest human safety
study lasted only 18 weeks.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.000
We needed better
human data.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:55.140
Since the largest rat study
highlighted lymphomas and leukemias,

00:06:55.140 --> 00:07:00.120
the NIH-AARP study tracked
blood cancer diagnoses…

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:03.840
and higher levels
of aspartame intake

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.880
were not associated
with the risk of cancer.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:09.240
It’s a massive study,
but was criticized

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:12.060
for only evaluating relatively
short-term exposure—

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:14.700
people were only
studied for 5 years.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:17.220
Hey—better than 18 weeks.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.340
But how about 18 years?

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.600
All eyes then
turned to Harvard,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.820
which started following
the health and diets

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.820
of medical professionals since before
aspartame even came on the market.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.240
In the most comprehensive
long-term population study

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:34.740
to evaluate the
association

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.500
between aspartame intake
and cancer risk in humans,

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:41.340
they did find an association
between both diet soda

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.940
and total aspartame intake and the
risks of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:46.860
and multiple myeloma in men

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.960
and leukemia in
both men and women.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.340
OK, but why more cancer
in men than women?

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.360
A similar result was found for
pancreatic cancer and diet soda.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.920
Not soda in general,

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.340
in fact the only sugar tied
to pancreatic cancer risk

00:08:02.340 --> 00:08:03.900
was the milk
sugar, lactose.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.300
It was the diet soda.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.420
So the male/female
discrepancy

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.820
could have just been
a statistical fluke,

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:14.160
but they decided to
dig a little deeper.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.780
Aspartame is broken
down into methanol,

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.600
and the methanol is
turned into formaldehyde,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.318
a documented human carcinogen.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:26.280
The same enzyme that
detoxifies regular alcohol

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:30.660
is the same enzyme that
converts methanol to formaldehyde.

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:33.000
Is it possible men
just have higher levels

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.620
of this enzyme
than women?

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.400
Yes. That’s why women get
higher blood alcohol levels

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:39.960
drinking the same
amount of alcohol.

00:08:40.860 --> 00:08:43.560
If you look at liver samples
from men and women,

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.220
there’s significantly greater
enzyme activity in the men.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.060
So maybe that explains the
increased cancer risk in men—

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.800
the higher conversion rates
from aspartame to formaldehyde.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.360
But how do we
test it though?

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.620
Ethanol—regular alcohol—

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.560
competes with methanol for
this same enzyme’s attention.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.500
In fact regular alcohol is
actually used as an antidote

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.480
for methanol poisoning.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.760
So men who
don’t drink

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:11.760
may have higher formaldehyde
conversion rates from aspartame

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:13.800
if this whole formaldehyde
theory is correct.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:17.220
And indeed, consistent
with this line of reasoning,

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:19.200
it was the men that
drank the least alcohol

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.860
that appeared to have the
greatest cancer risk from aspartame.

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:26.760
A third cohort study
has since been published

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.040
and found no increased
lymphoma risk

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.660
associated with
diet soda

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.880
during a ten year
follow-up period.

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:36.180
So no risk detected
in the 18 week study,

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:38.820
the 5 year study or
the 10 year study,

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:40.620
only in the
18 year study.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:42.180
What should we
make of all this?

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.340
Some have called for a re-evaluation
of the safety of aspartame.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:50.700
The horse is kind of
"out of the barn" at this point

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:53.880
with 34 million pounds of
the stuff produced annually,

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.880
but that doesn’t mean
we have to eat it,

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:01.919
especially, perhaps, pregnant
women and children.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:06.240
Finally today, the natural plant-based
sweeteners stevia and monk fruit

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:10.756
are pitted head-to-head against
aspartame and Splenda.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.380
A number of artificial sweeteners have

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.120
been FDA-approved in North America,

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:17.040
including aspartame and sucralose

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.000
(sold as Splenda).

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.320
But, there are also

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.060
natural high-intensity sweeteners

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:22.380
found in plants.

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:25.140
The global market for non-nutritive

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:27.720
sweeteners in general, these non-caloric

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:29.760
sweeteners, is in the billions,

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.440
including all the artificial ones—

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:34.920
and two natural ones
extracted from plants:

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.900
stevia and monk fruit.

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:39.360
I’ve done a video about stevia;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:40.920
what about monk fruit?

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:44.700
The fruits of Luo Han
Guo—monk fruit in Chinese—

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:47.220
have evidently been used for hundreds

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.680
of years as a natural
sweetener and folk medicine.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.120
The non-caloric sweet taste
comes from mogrosides,

00:10:54.120 --> 00:10:57.240
a group of cucurbitane-type
triterpene glycosides that

00:10:57.240 --> 00:10:59.160
make up about 1% of the fruit,

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:00.720
and are like hundreds of times

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:01.740
sweeter than sugar.

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:04.680
The mixed mogrosides
have been estimated

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.600
to be about 300 times as sweet

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.600
as table sugar, such that an 80% extract

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:12.000
was nearly 250 times sweeter than sugar.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.900
If you read reviews in
Chinese natural medicine journals,

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:17.640
you’ll see pronouncements like this:

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.620
monk fruit has been shown to have

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.900
anti-coughing effects, anti-asthma,

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:23.160
anti-oxidation, liver-protection,

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.260
blood sugar-lowering, immuno-regulation,

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.120
and anti-cancer.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:28.920
But, what they don’t tell you up front

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.380
is that they’re talking about reducing

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.140
ammonia-induced mouse coughs.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:36.600
A natural food sweetener with

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.340
anti-pancreatic cancer properties?

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:40.440
Monk fruit “may be used for daily

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:42.120
consumption as an additive in foods

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.580
and drinks to prevent or
treat pancreatic cancer.”

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:48.060
Yeah, maybe in your pet mouse.

00:11:48.960 --> 00:11:50.460
And, the anti-proliferative activity

00:11:50.460 --> 00:11:52.140
of monk fruit in colorectal cancer

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:53.940
and throat cancer was on colorectal

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.040
and throat cancer cells in a petri dish.

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:58.800
Now, they did show mogrosides killing

00:11:58.800 --> 00:12:02.100
off colorectal cancer cells
and throat cancer cells,

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.440
and our digestive tract could be

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.120
directly exposed to these compounds

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.940
if we ate them, but what’s missing?

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:12.840
Right, they didn’t test it
against normal cells.

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.120
You could pee in a petri dish

00:12:15.120 --> 00:12:16.560
and kill off cancer cells.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:17.640
The whole point is to find something

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:19.020
that kills off cancer but

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:20.520
leaves normal cells alone,

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:22.080
something that they weren’t

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:22.980
able to show here.

00:12:24.240 --> 00:12:27.300
Are there any human
studies on monk fruit?

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.040
No, …

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:31.680
until now.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.140
Owing to the rapidly growing popularity

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:35.700
of natural plant-based sweeteners,

00:12:35.700 --> 00:12:37.140
they thought it would be of interest

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:39.000
to determine whether natural sweeteners

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.100
would be a healthier alternative

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.320
to sugar or artificial sweeteners.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:45.480
So, they randomized people to drink

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:46.920
an aspartame sweetened beverage

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.200
versus monk fruit sweetened,

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.520
versus stevia

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:51.660
versus table sugar.

00:12:52.380 --> 00:12:54.360
And then they measured blood sugars

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.240
over 24 hours, and there was

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:59.880
no significant difference found

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.740
between any of them.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:03.420
But, wait a second.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:06.960
The sugar group was given
16 spoonfuls of sugar,

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:08.520
the amount of added sugar in a

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:10.080
20-ounce bottle of Coke.

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:12.960
So, the other three groups consumed

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.360
16 less spoonfuls of sugar

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:19.080
and still had the same
average blood sugars?

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.300
But, table sugar causes a big

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:22.200
blood sugar spike.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:22.860
Here it is; I’ll show you.

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:24.720
Drink that bottle of sugar water

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.120
with its 20 sugar cubes worth of sugar,

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.940
and your blood sugars jump 40 points

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.080
over the next hour.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.060
Whereas, you give them an aspartame

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:34.380
sweetened beverage, or monk fruit,

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:37.200
or stevia, and nothing happens,

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:39.360
which is what you’d expect, right?

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:41.400
These are non-caloric sweeteners,

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:42.000
no calories.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:44.580
It’s just like you’re drinking water,

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:45.120
right?

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:48.840
So, how could your daily blood sugar

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.180
values average out the same?

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:53.520
The only way that could happen is if

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:56.160
the non-calorie sweeteners maybe made

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.440
your blood sugar spikes worse

00:13:58.440 --> 00:14:00.420
somehow later in the day?

00:14:00.420 --> 00:14:02.280
Look what happens when you give people

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.320
Splenda mixed with sugar water.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:07.020
You get a greater blood sugar spike,

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:09.840
a greater insulin spike chugging the

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:11.520
sugar with sucralose than without,

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:13.740
even though Splenda alone causes

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:14.940
no spike of its own.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:18.240
So, does aspartame do the same thing?

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:19.380
At the one-hour mark,

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.600
they fed people a regular lunch.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:24.600
And, so, the blood sugars went back up

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:25.860
and down as they normally

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:26.820
would after a meal.

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:28.800
Not spiking as high as

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:29.820
drinking straight sugar water,

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:31.200
just a gentle up and down.

00:14:31.200 --> 00:14:33.600
OK, but that was in the group that

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:35.280
drank the sugar an hour before.

00:14:35.280 --> 00:14:37.440
In the group that drank the aspartame,

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:39.960
even though their blood sugars didn’t rise

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:42.060
at the time, an hour later at lunch,

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:45.600
they shot up higher, as if the person

00:14:45.600 --> 00:14:47.220
had just drank a bottle of soda.

00:14:48.060 --> 00:14:50.820
OK, but what about the natural sweeteners,

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:52.140
stevia and monk fruit?

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:54.300
Same thing.

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:57.060
Same exaggerated blood sugar spike

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:59.640
to a regular meal taken an hour later.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.940
So, you can see how it all equals out

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:04.500
in terms of average blood sugars

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:05.760
even though in these three

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.320
non-caloric sweetener groups,

00:15:07.320 --> 00:15:10.320
they took in 16 spoonfuls less sugar,

00:15:10.980 --> 00:15:13.440
at least in part because they ate more.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.560
After drinking a Diet Coke,
you’re more likely

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.900
to eat more at your next meal than

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.340
drinking a regular Coke.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:23.580
In fact, so much more
that the energy 'saved'

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.620
from replacing sugar with non-caloric

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:28.260
sweeteners was fully compensated for

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:29.520
at a subsequent meal;

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:32.880
hence, no difference in total daily

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.380
calorie intake was found.

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.020
The sugar-sweetened beverage led to

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:39.600
large spikes in both blood
sugar and insulin,

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:41.520
whereas these responses were higher

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:42.660
for the three other beverages

00:15:42.660 --> 00:15:44.520
following the lunch later.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.100
So, when it came to calorie intake,

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.680
or blood sugars, or insulin spikes,

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:53.814
they were all just as bad.

